# 3. MAKE THERMOS CONTINUOUSLY STATE-AWARE

The application must know what is happening throughout the simulation, not just when a user clicks a button.

Implement a recurring state-evaluation cycle.

On each simulation step:

1. Advance the simulation engine by the configured time increment.
2. Update the temperatures and workloads of all twelve racks.
3. Update sensor availability and estimated measurements.
4. Generate or refresh temperature forecasts using the existing trained model.
5. Calculate thermal risks and uncertainty margins.
6. Check cooling capacity and resource constraints.
7. Check workload placement, migration possibilities, and deadlines.
8. Update heat-recovery feasibility and energy accounting.
9. Detect new incidents.
10. Update existing incidents and their severity.
11. Identify incidents that have been resolved.
12. Evaluate interactions between incidents.
13. Generate candidate responses.
14. Rank and coordinate responses using the existing controller logic and explicit safety constraints.
15. Record decisions and outcomes.
16. Publish the updated state to the visual interface.

Use the actual simulation clock. Do not base scientific decisions on arbitrary browser timeouts.

Keep animation timing separate from physical simulation timing. A visual animation may run quickly, but it must represent the actual simulated action and its consequences.

The system must be able to pause, resume, step forward, reset, and inspect historical states without losing consistency.

