# 14. PERFORMANCE AND SIMULATION SPEED

The application must demonstrate responsiveness when several incidents are active.

Implement efficient state updates:
- Reuse the existing simulation engine.
- Avoid running duplicate predictions for the same state unless needed.
- Separate simulation calculations from rendering.
- Avoid recomputing unrelated components on every frame.
- Batch UI updates when appropriate.
- Use deterministic event processing.
- Avoid excessive particle effects or animations.
- Ensure pausing the simulation also pauses physical state progression.

If the existing model is inexpensive enough to evaluate synchronously, keep the architecture simple. If performance testing identifies bottlenecks, optimise the measured bottleneck rather than adding unnecessary infrastructure.

Measure the actual execution time of simulation steps and scenario runs.

Display simulation speed separately from wall-clock execution time.

Do not advertise that THERMOS is faster than competing systems unless a valid benchmark supports that claim.

The goal is to demonstrate how quickly the existing prototype can evaluate and coordinate responses within its tested scope.

