# 15. ACCEPTANCE TESTS

Do not declare completion until these tests have been performed.

### State awareness
- The facility state remains consistent across the scene, incident panel, controller, and timeline.
- Rack temperatures and workloads update from the actual engine.
- The system summary reflects current conditions rather than static text.

### Multi-event operation
- Several different incidents can be active simultaneously.
- Multiple racks can be affected at the same time.
- Duplicate alerts are suppressed or updated consistently.
- Related incidents can be linked.
- Incident severity changes as conditions change.

### Coordinated control
- The controller evaluates the facility as a whole.
- Compatible actions can be grouped into a response plan.
- Conflicting actions are detected and handled explicitly.
- Water, electricity, workload, and thermal consequences are accounted for.
- Failed actions trigger reassessment instead of false success.

### User interaction
- The judge can inspect individual racks during an active compound incident.
- Interactive mode requests approval before applying the proposed intervention.
- Demonstration mode clearly identifies automatically authorised actions.
- Pause, resume, reset, and timeline inspection work correctly.

### Scientific validity
- Predictions use the existing trained model.
- Sensor failure increases uncertainty according to the existing logic.
- Thermal responses come from the simulation engine.
- Heat-recovery accounting remains physically interpretable.
- Results are not fabricated to make THERMOS appear successful.

### Reliability
- The existing test suite still passes or any regression is explained and corrected.
- The compound-stress scenario is reproducible.
- The application runs without paid services or unnecessary API dependencies.
- The interface remains usable during concurrent incidents.

